.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt GENETLINK 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm genetlink
.Nd generic netlink families, requests and multicast events
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In linux/netlink.h
.In linux/genetlink.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_NETLINK" "SOCK_RAW" "NETLINK_GENERIC"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A generic netlink socket uses protocol 16 of
.Dv AF_NETLINK .
.Dv SOCK_RAW
and
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM
have the same behavior.
Any user may open a socket and discover families; an operation marked for
network administration needs privilege in the network namespace.
.Pp
Each request begins with
.Vt struct nlmsghdr ,
whose type is the family ID, followed by the four-byte
.Vt struct genlmsghdr :
a command byte, a version byte and two reserved bytes.
A family's private header follows, padded to four bytes, then its attributes.
An attribute has a native-endian 16-bit length, including its four-byte
header, a 16-bit type and its payload, padded to four bytes.
The type's high bits mark a nest or network byte order.
Unknown attribute IDs are skipped; a truncated attribute, repeated known
attribute or value outside its policy is
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
Set
.Dv NLM_F_REQUEST
and choose a sequence number.
Replies repeat that sequence number and use the socket's assigned port ID;
the request's claimed port ID does not select its recipient.
.Xr recv 2
reads one datagram, which may contain several complete messages.
.Sh FAMILY DISCOVERY
The controller,
.Ql nlctrl ,
has ID
.Dv GENL_ID_CTRL
(16) and version 2.
Send
.Dv CTRL_CMD_GETFAMILY
(3) with a terminated
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_FAMILY_NAME
or a 16-bit
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_FAMILY_ID .
The name takes precedence when both are present.
A family not registered is
.Er ENOENT .
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
the request lists the controller and every registered family.
.Pp
Each description is
.Dv CTRL_CMD_NEWFAMILY
(1), with the family's ID, name, version, private header size and maximum
attribute ID in
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_FAMILY_ID ,
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_FAMILY_NAME ,
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_VERSION ,
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_HDRSIZE
and
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_MAXATTR .
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_OPS
contains a nested entry per operation, with its command ID and flags:
administrative privilege (1), a single request handler (2), a dump handler
(4) and an attribute policy (8).
.Dv CTRL_ATTR_MCAST_GROUPS
contains nested entries with a terminated group name and its global
32-bit group number.
.Sh MULTICAST
Join groups 1 through 32 with the corresponding bits of
.Va sockaddr_nl.nl_groups
at
.Xr bind 2 .
The options
.Dv NETLINK_ADD_MEMBERSHIP
(1) and
.Dv NETLINK_DROP_MEMBERSHIP
(2), at level
.Dv SOL_NETLINK
(270), join or leave a group by its integer number, including numbers
above 32.
A group must be registered; zero, a negative number or an unknown group is
.Er EINVAL .
Membership is idempotent.
.Pp
Every member in the first network namespace receives its own copy.
A socket whose queue would exceed 64 KiB misses that event and its next
receive reports
.Er ENOBUFS ;
subsequent receives read the datagrams already queued.
Other members continue to receive.
.Dv NETLINK_NO_ENOBUFS
(5), set to a nonzero integer, suppresses the error but does not prevent
dropped events.
A consumer that loses events must obtain a new dump.
.Sh DUMPS AND ERRORS
A dump carries
.Dv NLM_F_MULTI
on each message and ends with
.Dv NLMSG_DONE ,
whose 32-bit payload is zero on success or a negative error number.
Read until that terminal message; a dump may span several datagrams.
It serves as a successful dump's acknowledgement.
Handlers run during the send, and the datagrams wait in the socket's queue.
.Pp
A failed request receives
.Dv NLMSG_ERROR
with a negative error number followed by the original request.
.Dv NLM_F_ACK
also requests this message on success, with an error number of zero.
A success echoes only the original header.
.Dv NETLINK_CAP_ACK
(10), set to a nonzero integer, also limits an error's echo to the header;
.Dv NLM_F_CAPPED
marks the shortened echo.
.Pp
.Dv NETLINK_EXT_ACK
(11), set to a nonzero integer, permits explanatory attributes after the
aligned echo.
.Dv NLM_F_ACK_TLVS
marks their presence:
.Dv NLMSGERR_ATTR_MSG
is a terminated explanation and
.Dv NLMSGERR_ATTR_OFFS
is the byte offset of an offending attribute from the request's start.
Neither attribute is required on every error.
.Sh LIMITS
A generic data datagram is at most 32448 bytes.
An uncapped error may also echo the complete request of up to 65536 bytes.
A registered family has at most 4096 attribute IDs and a name of at most
15 bytes.
The controller reports registered families but sends no notifications
when families are registered or removed.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr setsockopt 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr netlink 7
